{"page_content": "ACME/Flame Design Final Report (2023) \n \n6 April 2023 \n50 \n \n \nbecome \u2018hot\u2019 (i.e., dead) over time.  While these bright spots are addressed in image post-\nprocessing, they are a degradation of the image quality. \n \nACME\u2019s Mass Flow Controllers (MFCs) used to control the flow of fuel, nitrogen, and \noxidizer did not always performed adequately, where the flow rates were not always \nappropriately steady or at the intended value.  The resulting effects on the flame behavior \nare tentatively assumed to be minor, but this still needs to be assessed. \n \nThe ACME camera is not of sufficient quality to demonstrate the novel aspects of the \nexperiments to the public, but it appears to have been satisfactory from a science \nperspective. \n \nIt is possible that the tests were subtly affected by the acceleration environment, but \nthere was no obvious indication of such effects.  However, the Flame Design data has not \nbeen correlated with available measurements from the Space Acceleration Measurement \nSystem (SAMS).  Although the Combustion Integrated Rack (CIR) is equipped with a \nPassive Rack Isolation System (PaRIS), it was not used for ACME because of the significant \nneed for crew time that would have resulted.  \n \nRegardless of the shortcomings discussed above, a great wealth of experimental data has \nbeen secured and Flame Design\u2019s first round is considered to be at least minimally \nsuccessful despite the imperfect symmetry.  Depending on the results of the data \nprocessing, the round may rate as highly successful. \n \nSimilarly, a great wealth of experimental data was secured during Flame Design\u2019s second \nround of testing. Flame Design\u2019s second round is considered to be at least minimally \nsuccessful, and depending on the results of data processing, may be rated as highly \nsuccessful. \n \nJ. Status of Data Analysis  \nThe raw non-imaging data acquired from round 1 of Flame Design testing (e.g., gas flow \nrates, burner surface temperature, photomultiplier tube measurements of flame \nemissions, far-field gas temperatures, and pressures) has been converted to engineering \nunits.  All the raw (hobj) images obtained from the ACME camera have been successfully \nconverted to tiff files for further analysis.  Thin filament pyrometry is being used to obtain \nfiber temperature measurements in ACME camera images that have SiC fibers inserted in \nthe flame.  Soot temperature measurements using two-color ratio pyrometry are being", "metadata": {"source": "PSI-10_Reports_FinalRpt_ACMEFlameDesign.pdf", "page": 50}}